WEBVTT

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:08.700
Chapter 1. The Man Who Came After Moses.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:26.025
The last time Joshua had argued that Israel should enter the Promised Land, the people had nearly killed him. That was forty years ago. He had been one of twelve men sent to investigate Canaan, the country God had promised to Abraham's descendants.

00:00:26.205 --> 00:00:47.580
Their assignment was to examine the land, assess its inhabitants and defenses, and report back. What followed was one of the great catastrophes of Israelite history. Ten of the scouts returned convinced that the country was too dangerous to enter. Their report spread fear through the camp. The people panicked.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:01:08.560
Some demanded to return to Egypt, where they had been slaves. Joshua and Caleb argued that Israel could take the land, not because its inhabitants were weak, but because God had promised it to them. According to the Torah, the assembly threatened to stone the two men. The consequences were enormous.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:30.340
The generation that had escaped Egypt would die in the wilderness. Joshua and Caleb were among the few adults from the Exodus permitted to enter the land. Four decades passed. And now the nation was back where that failed mission should have led it. Joshua was in command. There was only one problem. Moses was dead.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:53.295
The succession. It is difficult to overstate what Moses meant to Israel. He had confronted Pharaoh and led the Exodus. He had stood at Sinai when the Torah was given. He had judged disputes, faced rebellions, established institutions and pleaded with God on behalf of a people who repeatedly tested him.

00:01:53.475 --> 00:02:15.850
At 120, Moses died east of the Jordan. The Torah says that his eyesight and strength had not failed. He had seen the land from Mount Nebo but would not enter it, and his burial place remained unknown. The Israelites mourned him for thirty days. Then the mourning ended, and the mission remained unfinished.

00:02:16.030 --> 00:02:33.805
Israel was still outside the land. Joshua had long been prepared to succeed him. He had served Moses and been publicly appointed in his place: Moses had laid his hands upon him, and the Torah describes Joshua as filled with a spirit of wisdom.

00:02:33.985 --> 00:02:56.960
Yet there was an obvious difference between having Moses' endorsement and having Moses at his side. Ahead lay a river crossing, warfare against the land's inhabitants, settlement and the eventual division of territory among the tribes. Moses had led the Israelites out of slavery. Joshua had to lead them into a home.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:17.415
After Moses' death, God spoke to Joshua. “Moses, My servant, is dead. Now arise. Cross this Jordan — you and all this people — into the land I am giving the Israelites.” The forty years of wandering were over. There would be no more waiting for Moses. The promise and the problem.

00:03:17.595 --> 00:03:28.370
God began with a sweeping description of the promised territory. Every place the Israelites set foot would be given to them, as He had told Moses.

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:47.375
Its boundaries were described from the wilderness and Lebanon toward the Euphrates, through the territory identified with the Hittites, and west to the Great Sea — the Mediterranean, in the direction of sunset. This was not a report of territory already controlled.

00:03:47.555 --> 00:04:06.630
Between Joshua and that promise stood other peoples, their settlements and defenses, and the work of establishing Israel in the land. God told Joshua that no one would be able to stand against him throughout his life. The presence that had accompanied Moses would accompany him too.

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:27.933
“I will not fail you or abandon you.” Then came the command that would define the chapter: חזק ואמץ. Be strong and courageous. Joshua was to lead Israel into the inheritance sworn to their ancestors. But strength in battle was only part of the responsibility.

00:04:28.113 --> 00:04:51.338
God repeated the command, this time with an unexpected emphasis. Joshua must be especially strong and courageous in keeping the entire Torah commanded through Moses, turning from it neither right nor left. The Torah was to remain on his lips; he was to engage with it day and night and put its instructions into practice.

00:04:51.518 --> 00:05:11.293
That fidelity, God said, was the foundation of a successful path. The man preparing to lead a military campaign was being told that his authority did not place him above the law. God repeated the command a third time. “Have I not commanded you? Be strong and courageous.

00:05:11.473 --> 00:05:17.573
Do not be afraid or dismayed, for the Lord your God is with you wherever you go.”

00:05:18.273 --> 00:05:39.983
Notice the distinction in the Hebrew. In verse 6, God says חזק ואמץ because Joshua must bring the people into their inheritance. In verse 7, the command becomes רק חזק ואמץ מאד — be very strong and courageous — but its subject is the observance of Torah.

00:05:40.163 --> 00:06:02.505
In verse 8, the word והגית calls for continuing engagement, traditionally understood in the sense of meditation or recitation. The book establishes its priorities before its first military operation: Joshua must possess the resolve to govern others, but also the discipline to govern himself.

00:06:03.205 --> 00:06:24.455
Three days. Joshua's first public action was practical. He ordered the officers responsible for transmitting instructions throughout the Israelite camp to tell the people to prepare provisions. Within three days they were to cross the Jordan and begin taking possession of the land.

00:06:24.635 --> 00:06:44.335
Consider how much had changed in a few sentences. For decades Israel had been a people in transit. The wilderness had been its home without ever being its intended destination. Now the old promise had become a logistical order: get ready to cross. But a problem remained.

00:06:44.515 --> 00:06:51.765
Not everyone in Israel needed to cross the Jordan to receive land. Some already had theirs.

00:06:51.945 --> 00:07:13.645
The tribes who already had their land. The tribes of Reuben and Gad, along with half the tribe of Manasseh, had been assigned territory east of the river. The arrangement began during Moses' lifetime. The eastern country offered good pasture, and Reuben and Gad possessed substantial herds.

00:07:13.825 --> 00:07:35.775
They asked to make their homes there rather than receive their inheritance west of the Jordan. From their perspective, the request made practical sense. From Moses' perspective, it threatened to become a national disaster. “Shall your brothers go to war while you remain here?” he demanded, as recorded in Numbers 32.

00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:52.905
Moses remembered the scouts. He remembered what had happened when Israel had lost the will to enter Canaan. If some tribes settled comfortably while their brothers fought, would the others lose heart all over again? The tribes offered a compromise.

00:07:53.085 --> 00:08:14.610
Their families and livestock would remain east of the river, but their fighting men would cross with the rest of Israel. They would help until their fellow Israelites received their own inheritance. Only then would they return permanently to the territory already allotted to them. Moses accepted the arrangement.

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:35.990
But Moses was dead now. And Joshua needed that promise to survive him. He spoke directly to Reuben, Gad and half of Manasseh. “Remember what Moses, the servant of the Lord, commanded you.” God had given these tribes land and rest. Their wives, children and livestock could stay east of the river.

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:57.045
The fighting men, however, were to cross armed, at the front of their fellow Israelites, and help them. They were not free to return permanently until the other tribes possessed their inheritance and had received rest as well. Only then could they go back east toward the sunrise, to the land Moses had given them.

00:08:57.225 --> 00:09:15.350
Joshua was not making a new deal. He was enforcing the old one. But much more was at stake than the fulfillment of a military obligation. Israel could not be a nation if its tribes abandoned one another the moment their individual needs had been met.

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:23.405
The security of Reuben, Gad and half of Manasseh was not permission to let everyone else fight alone.

00:09:24.105 --> 00:09:44.402
The Hebrew word here is מנוחה, usually translated as “rest.” In this setting, it is not merely a break from work. It points to the security of settlement after uncertainty and conflict. Joshua’s demand is that the eastern tribes help secure that rest for their brothers.

00:09:44.582 --> 00:09:57.857
The full bargain appears in Numbers 32 and is reiterated in Deuteronomy 3. Those earlier passages explain why Joshua is invoking Moses rather than negotiating fresh terms.

00:09:58.557 --> 00:10:14.307
The answer. The eastern tribes did not refuse. “Everything you command us, we will do,” they answered. “Wherever you send us, we will go.” They pledged to listen to Joshua as they had listened to Moses.

00:10:14.487 --> 00:10:32.487
But they added a qualification, or perhaps a hope: “Only may the Lord your God be with you, as He was with Moses.” The words recognized both Joshua's authority and the divine presence on which Israel's leadership depended. Then the pledge hardened.

00:10:32.667 --> 00:10:54.192
Anyone who rebelled against Joshua's command or refused to obey his instructions, they declared, would be put to death. It was an emphatic statement of allegiance, not an execution: no one is put to death in this chapter. And the tribes ended with words Joshua had already heard three times from God.

00:10:54.372 --> 00:11:15.122
“Only be strong and courageous.” The chapter began with the death of Moses. Joshua had heard the command to cross the river and the assurance that he would not be abandoned. He had directed the camp to prepare and held the eastern tribes to their old commitment. Now they had acknowledged his leadership.

00:11:15.302 --> 00:11:36.327
The Jordan still lay ahead. No Israelite city had been captured in Canaan; the campaign had not yet begun. But the people who could have stayed securely east of the river had agreed to march west. Forty years earlier, Joshua had tried to persuade Israel to enter the land, and the people had refused.

00:11:36.507 --> 00:11:39.182
This time, he would lead them.

00:11:40.082 --> 00:11:43.007
Chapter 2. The Woman in the Wall.

00:11:44.107 --> 00:11:59.282
The first people Joshua sent across the Jordan were not soldiers. They were two men whose names the text never gives. Joshua sent them secretly from Shittim, with a particular instruction: look at the land, especially Jericho.

00:11:59.462 --> 00:12:20.962
Forty years earlier, twelve scouts had returned with a report so frightening that Israel refused to enter Canaan. Now, with the river crossing imminent, Joshua sent only two. His choice of secrecy mattered. This was not another national referendum on whether Israel had the courage to proceed.

00:12:21.142 --> 00:12:36.517
It was reconnaissance. They entered Jericho and went to the house of a woman named Rahab, described in the Hebrew as a zonah, conventionally translated as a prostitute. Her house stood on the city's outer wall.

00:12:36.697 --> 00:12:52.822
A stranger might find a place like hers without attracting the questions that a stranger at an ordinary family home would invite. But the two men had not escaped notice. Someone reported their arrival to the king of Jericho.

00:12:53.002 --> 00:13:12.852
The king sent word to Rahab: the Israelite men who came to her house were spies. She was to bring them out. Rahab had already hidden them. She admitted that the men had visited, but denied knowing where they came from. They had left, she said, near dusk when the city gate was closing.

00:13:13.032 --> 00:13:32.957
If the king's men hurried after them, they might catch them. It was a lie, and it worked. The pursuers went toward the Jordan's crossing places. The gate shut behind them. The two Israelites were still inside the city, concealed on Rahab's roof beneath stalks of flax she had laid out there.

00:13:33.137 --> 00:13:50.762
Before they went to sleep, she climbed to the roof and explained herself. The Israelites might have expected to find confidence behind Jericho's defenses. Rahab described something very different. “I know that the Lord has given you the land,” she told them.

00:13:50.942 --> 00:14:08.042
“The fear of you has fallen upon us.” She knew the stories. The crossing of the sea after the Exodus. The defeat of Sihon and Og, the two Amorite kings on the far side of the Jordan. These events had reached Canaan before Joshua's army.

00:14:08.222 --> 00:14:26.822
Rahab said that when the people heard them, their courage dissolved. Then she made a declaration that reached beyond military calculation: Israel's God was God in heaven above and on earth below. Rahab wanted something in return for protecting the spies.

00:14:27.002 --> 00:14:45.777
She asked them to swear by their God that when Israel took Jericho, they would show her family the same loyalty she had shown them. Not just Rahab herself: she named her father, mother, brothers, and sisters, and asked protection for all their households and possessions.

00:14:45.957 --> 00:15:03.757
The men accepted, but not without conditions. Their lives for hers, they promised, if she kept their mission secret. When God delivered the land to Israel, they would deal faithfully with her. Their escape route was almost literally built into Rahab's home.

00:15:03.937 --> 00:15:24.437
A window opened onto the outside of the city wall. She lowered the men through it by a cord, then advised them not to head directly toward the Jordan. Pursuers would be waiting at the crossings. Instead, they should hide in the hills for three days, long enough for the search parties to return.

00:15:24.617 --> 00:15:44.042
Before they left, the spies made the terms of their oath unmistakable. Rahab had to hang a scarlet cord in the very window through which they escaped. When Israel attacked, her father, mother, siblings, and all who belonged to her father's household had to be gathered inside her house.

00:15:44.222 --> 00:16:04.847
Anyone who went out into the street would take responsibility for his own fate. The men would answer for harm done to anyone who remained inside. But if she revealed their mission, their promise was void. Rahab accepted every condition. She sent them away, and she tied the scarlet cord in the window.

00:16:05.027 --> 00:16:25.252
The two spies spent three days hiding in the hills while the pursuers searched the roads in vain. Then they came down, crossed the Jordan, and returned to Joshua. What they brought back was more than information about Jericho. It was evidence that the people on the other side were already afraid.

00:16:25.432 --> 00:16:45.107
“Truly,” they reported, “the Lord has given all the land into our hands. The inhabitants are melting before us.” Forty years earlier, scouts had returned from Canaan and terrified Israel. Joshua's two men returned from Canaan reporting that it was Canaan that was terrified.

00:16:45.287 --> 00:16:48.587
The contrast could hardly have been sharper.

00:16:49.287 --> 00:17:08.237
Rahab is often made into either a flawless heroine or a symbol of scandal. The chapter is more interesting than either simplification. It explicitly identifies her as a zonah and shows her protecting two men through deception, negotiating an oath, and confessing Israel's God.

00:17:08.417 --> 00:17:27.692
Later Jewish tradition, including discussions in the Talmud, develops her story much further; those traditions are not events narrated in Joshua 2. The scarlet cord is a practical sign of recognition in the text, but its conspicuous color also gives the story a memorable visual center.

00:17:27.872 --> 00:17:38.347
The oath's conditions matter: the Israelites' future obligation to Rahab is specific, not an unlimited guarantee detached from what she promised to do.

00:17:39.247 --> 00:17:41.722
Chapter 3. The River.

00:17:42.822 --> 00:18:02.897
Joshua rose early. Israel broke camp at Shittim and moved to the Jordan, where the people stayed before making the crossing. Everything depended on getting to the other side. The river was a natural boundary between the life Israel had known and the territory it meant to possess.

00:18:03.077 --> 00:18:23.102
And at harvest time, the Jordan swelled over its banks. The narrative makes a point of saying so: this was not an ordinary low-water crossing. After three days, officers passed through the camp with an instruction the people were not accustomed to hearing from an army preparing for advance.

00:18:23.282 --> 00:18:40.032
They were to watch for the Ark of the Covenant. When the priests of the tribe of Levi carried the Ark forward, the people were to follow. But they were not to crowd it. There had to be a distance of about two thousand cubits between the Ark and the rest of the nation.

00:18:40.212 --> 00:19:01.837
The reason was both practical and sacred. This was an unfamiliar route; the people needed to see where they were going. And the Ark was not an ordinary piece of military equipment. It represented the covenantal presence of God among Israel. Joshua told the people to prepare themselves in holiness.

00:19:02.017 --> 00:19:15.842
Tomorrow, he said, God would perform wonders among them. He instructed the priests to lift the Ark and go ahead. They did. Then God told Joshua what this crossing would mean for his leadership.

00:19:16.022 --> 00:19:34.072
From that day, God would begin to elevate Joshua in the eyes of Israel, so that the people would know the divine presence that had accompanied Moses had not ended with Moses' death. The priests carrying the Ark were to enter the river and stop at the edge of its waters.

00:19:34.252 --> 00:19:46.277
Joshua gathered the Israelites to hear the promise. The living God was among them, he said, and the conquest of the land was not a contest Israel would face alone.

00:19:46.457 --> 00:20:07.507
He named the peoples whose territories Israel was to inherit: Canaanites, Hittites, Hivites, Perizzites, Girgashites, Amorites, and Jebusites. He also ordered that one man be selected from each of Israel's twelve tribes. Then Joshua described the sign that would make the crossing possible.

00:20:07.687 --> 00:20:24.462
As soon as the feet of the priests bearing the Ark touched the Jordan, the flow from upstream would stop and gather in a heap. The people would cross on the riverbed. The priests went first. Their feet entered the swollen water. And the river stopped.

00:20:24.642 --> 00:20:38.692
The water coming down from the north gathered far upstream, at Adam near Zarethan. The flow toward the Sea of the Arabah—the Salt Sea—was cut off. Opposite Jericho, the riverbed opened.

00:20:38.872 --> 00:20:54.722
The priests remained where they had been commanded to stand, holding the Ark on dry ground in the middle of what had been the river. The Israelites passed around them, crossing on dry land until the whole nation had reached the western bank.

00:20:54.902 --> 00:21:14.002
The crossing had not involved an improvised ford or a desperate assault. In the story's own terms, it had depended on a deliberate act of God, witnessed by the people and enacted through the priests' obedience. For Joshua, the symbolism was unavoidable.

00:21:14.182 --> 00:21:33.907
Moses had led Israel through the sea when they escaped Egypt. Joshua had led them through the Jordan as they entered Canaan. Israel was about to learn that the successor's authority was not simply a title inherited from the past. The river was behind them now. Jericho was ahead.

00:21:34.607 --> 00:21:52.182
The Ark of the Covenant is central to this chapter. The people are told to leave a visible distance of roughly two thousand cubits, not to press forward as if this were a normal military column. The precise physical mechanism of the waters' stopping is not explained.

00:21:52.362 --> 00:22:10.312
The text supplies geographical detail—Adam, Zarethan, the Salt Sea—and explicitly states that the Jordan was in flood. Those details belong to the biblical account; describing a natural mechanism as established fact would go beyond what the passage says.

00:22:10.492 --> 00:22:19.017
The twelve selected men become important in the next chapter. Joshua 3 identifies them before it explains their task.

00:22:19.917 --> 00:22:23.617
Chapter 4. The Stones That Would Tell the Story.

00:22:24.717 --> 00:22:42.642
The people had crossed, but the priests were still standing in the riverbed with the Ark. God gave Joshua another command before the moment passed. He was to take the twelve men already chosen—one from each tribe—and send them to the place where the priests stood.

00:22:42.822 --> 00:22:58.172
Each man was to lift a stone from the bed of the Jordan and carry it on his shoulder to the place where Israel would camp that night. The task was deliberately physical. Twelve men, twelve stones, one from each tribe.

00:22:58.352 --> 00:23:11.952
The crossing belonged to the whole nation, and every tribe would have a place in its memorial. Joshua explained what the stones were for. One day, children would see them and ask what they meant.

00:23:12.132 --> 00:23:30.682
Their parents would answer that the waters of the Jordan had stopped before the Ark of the Covenant, and that Israel had crossed on dry ground. The stones were not merely decoration. They were a way to turn an event into a story that could survive the generation that witnessed it.

00:23:30.862 --> 00:23:50.312
The men did as they were told. They took twelve stones from the river and carried them to the place where Israel would spend the night. The chapter also reports another memorial: Joshua set up twelve stones in the Jordan itself, at the spot where the priests holding the Ark had stood.

00:23:50.492 --> 00:24:05.392
The writer says those stones remained there “to this day,” a reminder that the narrative is speaking to a later audience remembering the crossing. The priests did not move until every instruction had been carried out.

00:24:05.572 --> 00:24:23.747
Only then did the Ark leave the riverbed, with the people already on the western bank. Among those crossing armed were the warriors of Reuben, Gad, and half of Manasseh. Chapter 1 had ended with their promise to Joshua. Here the book reports that they kept it.

00:24:23.927 --> 00:24:39.152
Approximately forty thousand armed men from those tribes crossed toward the plains of Jericho, ready for war, while their families remained in the territory east of the Jordan. The event changed Joshua's standing.

00:24:39.332 --> 00:24:58.407
On that day, says the text, God magnified him before all Israel, and the people regarded him with the awe they had shown Moses. Then Joshua ordered the priests to come up out of the Jordan. As soon as their feet returned to dry land, the waters resumed their course.

00:24:58.587 --> 00:25:15.137
The river rose again over its banks as before. It was as though the crossing had opened and closed for a single purpose. The Israelites came out of the river on the tenth day of the first month. They camped at Gilgal, east of Jericho.

00:25:15.317 --> 00:25:31.292
There Joshua set up the twelve stones the men had carried from the riverbed. And he returned to the question the monument was meant to answer. When future children asked what these stones meant, their parents were to tell them about the crossing.

00:25:31.472 --> 00:25:50.797
The explanation would reach back beyond Joshua, to the sea God had dried before Israel at the Exodus. The Jordan was not to be remembered as an isolated marvel. It belonged to the same national story as the departure from Egypt. There were two audiences for the event.

00:25:50.977 --> 00:26:08.477
Israel's descendants were to learn that the covenant had carried their ancestors into the land. And the peoples of the earth were to recognize the power of Israel's God. A stone cannot make anyone remember. It can only give them something to ask about.

00:26:08.657 --> 00:26:12.657
Joshua had arranged for the question to outlive him.

00:26:13.357 --> 00:26:27.157
The chapter mentions two groups of twelve stones: stones carried out of the river to Gilgal and stones Joshua set up in the Jordan itself. Readers who recall only one monument miss a distinctive detail.

00:26:27.337 --> 00:26:41.537
It also supplies a date, the tenth day of the first month—Nisan—which matters because the next chapter places Passover only days away. And it records that the eastern tribes honored their earlier military pledge.

00:26:41.717 --> 00:26:50.392
Joshua's authority is established not only by a miracle but by visible compliance from people who could otherwise have stayed home.

00:26:51.292 --> 00:26:54.517
Chapter 5. The Day the Manna Stopped.

00:26:55.617 --> 00:27:11.917
The news crossed Canaan faster than an army could. West of the Jordan, Amorite kings heard that the river had dried before the Israelites. The Canaanite kings along the coast heard it too. The text says their courage failed.

00:27:12.097 --> 00:27:27.047
The news of the crossing had done something to the political balance before Joshua's forces fought their first battle in the land. And then Joshua ordered something that could only make sense in the context of a covenant.

00:27:27.227 --> 00:27:47.952
The Israelite men who had been born during the wilderness years were to be circumcised. God told Joshua to prepare flint knives and carry out the rite. The place became known as Gibeath-ha-araloth, the Hill of Foreskins. The explanation reaches back to Israel's forty years of wandering.

00:27:48.132 --> 00:28:05.357
The men of war who had left Egypt had been circumcised, but the next generation, born during the journey, had not. The earlier generation had died in the wilderness after refusing to listen to God. Their children had now taken their place.

00:28:05.537 --> 00:28:17.587
Joshua circumcised the men, and the camp stayed where it was while they recovered. This was not how an ordinary military commander might choose to begin a campaign.

00:28:17.767 --> 00:28:34.192
The river had been crossed and the enemy frightened, yet Israel paused to mark its identity through the covenant that had begun with Abraham. God told Joshua that the disgrace associated with Egypt had been rolled away from Israel.

00:28:34.372 --> 00:28:50.397
The place was called Gilgal—a name the text associates with the Hebrew verb for rolling away. The Israelites were still camped in the plains of Jericho when the fourteenth day of the first month arrived. They observed Passover.

00:28:50.577 --> 00:29:10.852
The festival was a remembrance of the night their ancestors had been freed from slavery. But this Passover came on the far side of the Jordan, in the land toward which the Exodus had been leading. On the day after the festival, they ate food grown in the land: unleavened bread and roasted grain.

00:29:11.032 --> 00:29:29.282
Then something happened that marked the end of an era more quietly than the crossing of the river. The manna stopped. For decades, according to the Torah, the people had survived on food supplied during their life in the wilderness. Now they were eating the produce of Canaan.

00:29:29.462 --> 00:29:48.412
The text is explicit: manna no longer fell, and the Israelites ate from the harvest of the land that year. The miraculous provision of the journey had ended because the people had reached the place where they could begin to live differently. Yet the conquest was only beginning.

00:29:48.592 --> 00:30:08.492
Near Jericho, Joshua looked up and saw a man standing opposite him with a drawn sword. Joshua approached and asked the question any commander would ask a stranger who appeared armed on the eve of battle. “Are you for us, or for our enemies?” The answer was unexpected. “No.

00:30:08.672 --> 00:30:29.372
I have come now as commander of the Lord's army.” Joshua fell face down and bowed. “What does my lord say to his servant?” The figure gave him an instruction that echoed an earlier moment in Moses' life. “Remove your sandal from your foot. The place where you stand is holy.” Joshua did so.

00:30:29.552 --> 00:30:50.202
The first battle was approaching. But the man carrying the drawn sword had not offered Joshua an ordinary military alliance. Before Joshua could ask how to take Jericho, he was reminded that the ground beneath him could be holy—and that Israel's commander was not the highest authority present.

00:30:50.902 --> 00:31:07.152
The pause for brit milah before battle is easy to miss if the story is reduced to a sequence of military victories. The chapter also situates Israel's first Passover in the land immediately before the cessation of manna.

00:31:07.332 --> 00:31:19.732
This is a transition from wilderness dependence to the agricultural life of Canaan. The command to remove a sandal strongly recalls Moses at the burning bush in Exodus 3.

00:31:19.912 --> 00:31:40.087
Joshua asks whether the armed figure is “for us or for our enemies,” but the answer is “No”—refusing the premise that the divine commander exists merely to join Joshua's side. Later interpreters explore the identity of the figure; the chapter itself identifies him as commander of the Lord's army.
